deck is still a 20-minute talk: many slides are single-click resets, not
mini-lectures.

## The four dynamic builds (built in this environment, timeline visual language; event-driven, advance on click)

**BUILD #1 — three-layer spine (2, 12, 20).** One shared time axis
1950→present, three trajectories drawn in sequence. S2: axis + empty tracks.
S12: ecological structure curve joined by the value curve; the sourced wording
is that roe landed/wholesale value peaked in the 1980s, stayed relatively high
to the mid-1990s, then dropped through 1995–2005 and again after 2008. The
horizontal offset is the management window. S20: cultural-service curve and
governance window complete the picture; all three breakpoints are visibly
non-coincident, and the window shades shut. One SVG, step index advances each
return. Wire-in: current `m1_stier_11` portfolio / synchrony track; DFO/CHN/
Parks Canada 2024 Rebuilding Plan Figs 31/32 value track — **use the Haida
Gwaii (2E) series, not the BC-coastwide curve (≈order of magnitude larger);
state which on the slide** (see `figs/PROVENANCE.md`); schematic cultural
track anchored to recent k'aaw closures.

**BUILD #2 — draggable hysteresis (14).** Phase portrait: x = driver
(predation pressure / cumulative mortality), y = system state, folded curve
(upper/lower stable branches, unstable middle). Drag driver right → dot falls
off the fold (collapse). Drag back left → dot does NOT retrace; stuck on lower
branch. Caption: *the driver is back where it started; the system is not.* Can
live inside the Build #1 artifact as the mechanism layer.

**BUILD #3 — portfolio comes apart (7).** Nine subpopulation biomass lines
fanned (independent) in early decades; on click time runs, post-1994 lines
braid into one strand; the on-screen counter shows the **current
`m1_stier_11`** metrics, not an unlabeled index. Wire-in: focal-9 posterior
series, ≈65-yr span, from `m1_stier_11`. **On-screen current numbers (cite
`Output/diagnostics/m1_stier_11_portfolio_metrics.md`):** recent synchrony
**0.63** all-11 / **0.70** focal-9; Simpson effective sections **3.26** /
**3.31**; recent-period top-three biomass share **84%**. **Motivation
annotations — label as Stier et al. 2020 (published, not the current refit):**
"~2.1× more stable than a homogeneous metapopulation" and "local harvest 65%
vs archipelago-wide 4%" (the scale-mismatch result). Full reconciliation:
`talk-usuk-forum-2026/Talk_Materials/numbers_provenance.md`.

**BUILD #4 — a bigger mouth is not a coefficient (10).** Year scrubber
1970→2025; stacked pressure/demand context updates live. Commercial removals
dominate early, then predator demand becomes a large ecological pressure field.
Wire-in: Doherty 2025 as a **WCVI** proof that mammal predation can dominate
natural mortality; HG predator-repo demand as the local scale context; Surma &
Pitcher 2015 and the current model ledger as the guardrail. Caption:
*large enough to matter, not yet identified enough to promote.*

## Charts (static, one idea, minimal ink)

- **S3 — the system we can measure.** Map of 11 fitted sections with a small
  model-contract strip: ambiguous zeros, two-era `q`, promoted baseline =
  `m1_stier_11`, focal-9 reporting sensitivity. This slide prevents later
  model questions from hijacking the story.
- **S5 — 1967 collapse.** Coastwide catch 1935–1972 (climb to ~240 kt;
  241→181→135 kt fall; shaded 1968–71 closure). One callout: warning signals
  were present and ignored. Quiet setup slide.
- **S6 — closure is not recovery.** Direct fishing removals fall to zero, but
  recent biomass remains concentrated and section recovery remains uneven.
  Use this to separate "stopped fishing" from "service recovered."
- **S8 — where the losses live.** Section action matrix or map/photo hybrid:
  Cumshewa and Louscoone = clean mechanism scrutiny; Skidegate/Laskeek/Rennell
  = portfolio erosion; Tasu/Naden = sparse-data sensitivity.
- **S9 — what can and cannot explain it.** Mechanism-menu panel: spatial /
  portfolio erosion and historical fishing pressure are the robust HG patterns;
  PDO and predators remain plausible but confounded; simple density dependence
  and simple predator-pit stories are weak for HG. Source the ranking from the
  model ledger, covariate readiness registry, and
  `docs/herring-non-recovery-hypotheses.md`.
- **S11 — Doherty replication boundary.** Small table: WCVI catch-at-age /
  size-at-age / predation mortality = model-structure reference; HG public data
  = source-traceable assessment inputs and proxies; current talk = bridge/gap
  table, not full catch-at-age replication.
- **S12 — biomass vs. value (keystone, fused with SPINE).** Use the sourced
  Rebuilding Plan wording: BC roe landed/wholesale value peaked in the 1980s,
  stayed relatively high until the mid-1990s, dropped during 1995–2005, and
  declined again from 2008-present. The point is the lag: aggregate value can
  stay high while ecological structure is already failing.
- **S17 — Strait of Georgia counterfactual.** Two-column matched-row panel
  (HG vs SoG): stock status, current biomass, residency/migration, predation
  structure, fishery history. The negative control immunizes against "you just
  picked a collapsed stock."
- **S18 — zero, still below the line.** 2025 forecast/reference-point context
  with no fishing. Source: DFO CSAS SR 2025/005; keep wording as scale/status
  comparison, not likelihood validation for our model.
- **Backup — egg vs adult harvest asymmetry.** Operating-space diagram (egg vs
  adult harvest, depletion contour; "fishery target" and "predator-protective"
  spaces overlapping) — Shelton et al. 2014. Economics bridge to Kubiszewski.

## Photo slides (source real imagery — placeholders won't survive this room)

- **S1 — title.** Full-bleed white-water spawn image/video with the title over
  the water, not in a card. This should make herring visually present before
  any model appears.
- **S4 — ten thousand years.** White-water spawn; then deep-time evidence
  (fine-screen archaeological stratigraphy, cedar herring rake). Numbers
  spoken: McKechnie et al. 2014 — 435,777 bones, 171 sites, herring 49% of all
  bones, 99% ubiquity, temporal variance < ±10% across the record.
- **S13 — named bays.** Photographs of Burnaby Narrows, Skidegate Inlet,
  Louscoone, paired with the roll-call TEXT. A dotted map is the weaker option.
- **S19 — co-governance.** No org chart. Use the official AMB/Gwaii Haanas
  photo as the safe main image, with the 2024 iináang|iinang rebuilding plan as
  document evidence. Athlii Gwaii / Lyell Island blockade and 2024 signing-news
  images are rights/label-sensitive backups. One line: six seats (3 federal, 3
  Haida), consensus not majority, first ecosystem-based rebuilding plan in BC.

## Text slides

- **S1 — title.** Timeline masthead language.
- **S13 — roll call (with the photo).** Closed sites as liturgy: Burnaby
  Narrows · Skidegate Inlet · Louscoone Inlet · Cumshewa · Selwyn · Rennell
  Sound · Hunter Point · Marble Island · Scudder Point · Juan Perez ·
  Skincuttle. Then: *functionally closed for traditional harvest for the first
  time in ~10,000 years.* Keep one TEK datum: **Teeshoshum** — "waters white
  with herring spawn," no documented spawning since 1998. (Gerrard 2014
  7.6%/decade curve = backup only.)
- **S15 — two systems, same data.** Stier et al. 2016 (*Conservation
  Letters*): 27 regional experts, two structurally distinct mental models,
  clustering unrelated to background/experience/affiliation; under simulated
  recovery clusters diverge 182% / 78% / 89% on reorganization, abundance
  change, whale increase; disagree on direction for pink & chum salmon. Text +
  at most two small contrasting network glyphs.
- **S20 — what changes Monday.** Three solution instructions, each tied to a
  slide already shown: measure spatial structure, manage exposure under
  uncertainty, and close the missing age/size/predator-exposure data gaps
  without waiting for a single solved mechanism.

## Design system

Use the **timeline artifact's** finished language: Crimson Pro / IBM Plex,
rust-and-marine palette, editorial masthead. Deck + timeline + spine read as
one object — no generic LTER scientific-figure styling. Shared SVG conventions;
the same six-domain color coding (climate, fishery, ecology, culture,
governance, science) carried from timeline → spine → builds; event-driven JS
for every animation; ruthless ink restraint.

## Closing positioning

Herring is the empirical spine. Two comparative systems sharpen it without
competing for airtime: **Moorea (MCR LTER)** COVID natural experiment (one
fast/clean slide testing the management-window concept, not a 2nd case study);
**kelp (SBC LTER)** as the fast-turnover *control* (recovers on a timescale
herring/reefs do not — demonstrates the mechanism), not a competing collapse.
For Session 5: S12 + egg/adult backup are the bridge to Kubiszewski's
ecological-economics frame. Dropping kelp as a primary system keeps clear water
from White's and Smale's talks.

## What got cut

Governance org chart → photographs. TEK contraction curve → backup (place name
out-performs the regression). Egg/adult → backup. Moorea/kelp comparative
systems → at most Q&A. 13 per-finding charts → 8 concise proof slides + 4
builds + image/text resets.

## Backup / likely-question slides

Build these after the 20 core slides. They should look like appendix cards:
one question as the title, one answer line, one proof object. Do not let them
compete with the main sequence.

| Backup | Likely question | Answer line | Asset/proof object |
|---|---|---|---|
| B1 | "Isn't this just predators?" | Predator demand is large enough to be a serious pressure, but the HG model does not yet promote a predator coefficient. | `predator_talk_brief.md`; `MASTER_HG_predation_AUDITED.pdf`; model ledger held-branch table |
| B2 | "How close are you to the Doherty analysis?" | We replicated the data-readiness logic and built a biomass-scale proxy bridge; full HG catch-at-age predation mortality remains data-limited. | `doherty-style-hg-gap-table.md`; `doherty-style-hg-replication-status.md` |
| B3 | "Are zeros real absences?" | Not by default. The baseline treats zeros/no-survey cells as ambiguous unless a survey was designed to establish absence. | `survey_coverage_zero_ambiguity.md`; model claim-control sheet |
| B4 | "Why not use the DFO recruitment table as independent juvenile data?" | Appendix recruitment/biomass outputs are assessment products, not independent juvenile-survey observations for validating our likelihood. | `doherty-style-hg-source-provenance.md`; DFO extraction notes |
| B5 | "How robust is the baseline model?" | `m1_stier_11` is the promoted Stier-aligned baseline; exact re-LOO resolved the one high-k point, and held branches did not beat calibration gates. | `latest_model_status.md`; `model_decision_ledger.md`; exact re-LOO note |
| B6 | "Could this be a three-year lag / recruitment return effect?" | Biologically plausible and worth testing, but current lag screens are audit targets, not promoted evidence. | `future_lag_negative_control_audit.md`; predator integration roadmap |
| B7 | "Why compare with Strait of Georgia?" | SoG is the negative control: not all BC herring systems share the HG collapse/recovery geometry. | `hg_dfo_sca_external_comparison.pdf`; DFO SR 2025/005 |
| B8 | "What should managers do without a solved mechanism?" | Manage exposure and preserve spatial structure while closing data gaps; uncertainty is not a reason to wait for a single coefficient. | Rebuilding Plan objectives; S20 final spine; Shelton egg/adult harvest backup |

